Declare _strdup in nslexer.l

`nslexer.l` calls `strdup`, which `"namespace.h"` rewrites to `_strdup`. That
works when the file's own includes are the first thing in the translation unit,
because `<string.h>` is then read *after* the rename and declares the internal
name.

Built with flex rather than NetBSD's own lex, it is not: flex's skeleton emits
`#include <string.h>` near the top of the generated `.c`, ahead of the `%{ %}`
block, so `strdup` is declared under its ordinary name and the later
`<string.h>` here is a no-op thanks to the header guard. `_strdup` is then
never declared:

    namespace.h:681:33: error: implicit declaration of function '_strdup'
    nslexer.l:78:32: error: assignment to 'char *' from 'int' ...

Both were warnings until GCC 14 made them errors. Declaring the renamed symbol
fixes the actual defect, rather than demoting the two diagnostics across all of
libc -- `-Wno-error=int-conversion` in particular is far too blunt to want on a
libc build, since it would hide genuine pointer/integer confusion everywhere
else.

This is not version-specific: `nslexer.l` is byte-identical in 9.2, 10, 11 and
-current. It depends on our flex, not on NetBSD's release.

--- a/lib/libc/net/nslexer.l
+++ b/lib/libc/net/nslexer.l
@@ -42,6 +42,14 @@
 #include <string.h>
 #include <syslog.h>
 
+/*
+ * flex's skeleton emits its own #include <string.h> above this block, so by
+ * the time "namespace.h" renames strdup to _strdup the declaration has
+ * already been pulled in under the original name, and including <string.h>
+ * again is a no-op thanks to its guard. Declare the renamed symbol.
+ */
+char *_strdup(const char *);
+
 #include "nsparser.h"
 
 %}
